/* ---------- attractions ---------- */
const ATTRACTIONS = [
  { img: 'bp-parliament', name: 'Hungarian Parliament', text: 'This iconic neo-Gothic masterpiece on the Danube is not only Hungary’s seat of government but also a symbol of national pride, renowned for its ornate interiors and the Crown of St. Stephen. Guided tours offer a glimpse into its lavish halls, and its riverside location provides some of the city\'s best photo opportunities.', how: 'You can reach the parliament from Amber Studios in about 28 minutes on foot. It is a beautiful walk alongside the Danube bank with unforgettable views towards Gellért Hill, the Budapest Castle, Fisherman\'s Bastion and Chain Bridge. Alternatively, take tram number 2 or 23 for a convenient ride that gets you there in just 16 minutes. Tram nr. 2 was featured in National Geographic as one of best tram rides for sightseeing in Budapest.' },
  { img: 'bp-shoes', name: '"Shoes on the Danube Bank" memorial', text: 'This moving memorial of iron shoes along the riverbank commemorates the victims of World War II, offering a poignant and reflective experience just steps from the Parliament. It is located 200 meters before you reach the Parliament on the Danube Bank.', how: 'Enjoy a 25 minutes scenic walk along the Danube with stunning views of Gellért Hill, Budapest Castle, Fisherman\'s Bastion, and the Chain Bridge. You can also take tram 2 or 23 for a quick 16-minute ride—tram 2 is recognized by National Geographic as one of the top sightseeing routes in Budapest.' },
  { img: 'bp-danube', name: 'The Danube riverside in Budapest', text: 'The Danube riverside is just 400 meters from Amber Studios—an easy 6-minute walk away. From the nearest point on the riverbank, you can enjoy stunning views of many of Budapest’s most iconic landmarks, including the Budapest University of Technology and Economics, Gellért Hill with the Liberty Statue, the historic Gellért Hotel and Baths, the majestic Buda Castle, the Matthias Church, the picturesque Fisherman’s Bastion, as well as the Freedom Bridge, Elisabeth Bridge, and the famous Chain Bridge.', how: 'This close proximity allows you to experience the beauty and charm of Budapest’s riverside and its remarkable cityscape within minutes of leaving your accommodation.' },
  { img: 'bp-castle', name: 'The Buda Castle and Castle District', text: 'Buda Castle—also known as the Royal Palace—is a grand historic complex perched atop Castle Hill on the Buda side of Budapest. Originally built in the 13th century and rebuilt several times, it has served as the residence of Hungarian kings and now houses the Hungarian National Gallery, the Budapest History Museum, and the National Széchényi Library. The Castle District (Várnegyed) surrounds Buda Castle and is a charming, UNESCO World Heritage-listed area known for its cobblestone streets, medieval and Baroque houses, and atmospheric squares. Key sights include the Matthias Church, Fisherman’s Bastion, and the famous catacombs of Budapest.', how: 'The iconic Chain Bridge is easily accessible from Amber Studios—a scenic 15-minute walk along the Danube riverside, or take tram number 2 from Március 15. tér for a picturesque 5-minute ride. Cross the bridge on foot and take the funicular up to the Castle District. Bus nr. 16 from Deák Ferenc tér (one metro stop from Ferenciek Square) also runs across the Chain Bridge up to Dísz tér in the Castle District.' },
  { img: 'bp-synagogue', name: 'The Budapest Synagogue', text: 'The Budapest Synagogue, officially known as the Dohány Street Synagogue or Great Synagogue, is the largest synagogue in Europe and the second largest in the world, with seating for nearly 3,000 people. Built between 1854 and 1859 in the Moorish Revival style, its striking architecture features twin towers with onion domes and richly decorated interiors. The complex also includes the Heroes’ Temple, a Jewish cemetery, a Holocaust memorial (the Tree of Life), and the Jewish Museum, which stands on the birthplace of Theodor Herzl.', how: 'You can reach the Great Synagogue from Amber Studios with an easy 14-minute walk. Simply head along Reáltanoda Street toward Astoria Square, then turn left to arrive at the synagogue.' },
  { img: 'bp-basilica', name: 'St. Stephen’s Basilica (Szent István-bazilika)', text: 'It is the largest church in Budapest and one of Hungary’s most significant religious and architectural landmarks. Named after Stephen I, the first King of Hungary, the basilica houses his mummified right hand, known as the Holy Right. Construction began in 1851 and was finally completed and consecrated in 1905. The basilica can accommodate up to 8,500 people. Visitors can climb 364 steps or take an elevator to the dome’s observation deck for some of the city’s best views—we highly recommend doing that.', how: 'About 20 minutes on foot through the city center, or take Metro Line 3 (the Blue Line) from Ferenciek Square to Deák Ferenc Square—just one stop. The entire journey takes approximately 15 minutes.' },
  { img: 'bp-andrassy', name: 'Andrássy út (Andrássy Avenue)', text: 'One of Budapest’s grandest and most historic boulevards, stretching about 2.5 km from the city center (Erzsébet Square) to Heroes’ Square and the City Park. Built in the late 19th century and recognized as a UNESCO World Heritage Site, the avenue is famous for its elegant Neo-Renaissance mansions, luxury boutiques, embassies, and cultural landmarks like the Hungarian State Opera House and the House of Terror Museum. The historic Millennium Underground (M1 metro), Europe’s first continental subway, runs beneath the avenue.', how: 'About 20 minutes on foot, or take Metro Line 3 (the Blue Line) from Ferenciek Square to Deák Ferenc Square—just one stop. Approximately 15 minutes in total.' },
  { img: 'bp-heroes', name: 'Heroes’ Square', text: 'Heroes’ Square (Hősök tere) is renowned for its grand monuments and historical significance. Located at the end of Andrássy Avenue, adjacent to City Park (Városliget), it serves as a symbolic gateway to the city’s cultural and historical heart. At the base of the column are equestrian statues representing the seven chieftains of the Magyar tribes who led the Hungarians into the Carpathian Basin in 896.', how: 'Take Metro Line 3 (Blue Line) from Ferenciek Square to Deák Ferenc Square. There, transfer to Metro Line 1 (Yellow Line)—Europe’s oldest metro line, opened in 1896—and ride to Hősök tere station.' },
];

/* ---------- shopping ---------- */
const SHOPPING = [
  { img: 'bp-christmas', name: 'Famous Christmas Markets in Budapest', text: 'Budapest’s Christmas markets are among the most celebrated in Europe, offering festive atmosphere, artisan crafts, and Hungarian specialties. They usually open in the middle of November and close by the end of the year, usually the 31 of December. The Christmas market on Vörösmarty Square marks the entrance to Váci Utca, creating a festive gateway to Budapest’s famous pedestrian street.' },
  { img: 'bp-fashion-street', name: 'Budapest Fashion Street', text: 'Budapest Fashion Street, officially known as Deák Ferenc utca, is the city’s premier shopping boulevard and a renowned destination for fashion, lifestyle, and urban culture. Located just steps from Vörösmarty Square and the Danube Promenade, Fashion Street boasts a wide variety of mid-range and luxury international fashion brands, as well as Hungarian boutiques and lifestyle stores. Flagship stores include Hugo Boss, Lacoste, Furla, Zara, Tommy Hilfiger, Nike, Lloyd, Massimo Dutti and many more. The street is also home to stylish cafés, restaurants, and two five-star hotels: The Ritz-Carlton Budapest and Kempinski Hotel Corvinus.' },
  { img: 'bp-market-hall', name: 'The Great Market Hall', text: 'The Great Market Hall (Nagyvásárcsarnok) is Budapest’s largest and oldest indoor market, located at the southern end of Váci Street on the Pest side near Liberty Bridge. Opened in 1897 and designed by architect Samu Pecz, the building is famous for its striking neo-Gothic entrance and colorful Zsolnay tiled roof.' },
  { img: 'bp-vaci', name: 'Váci Utca', text: 'Váci utca is Budapest’s most famous pedestrian shopping street, stretching from Vörösmarty Square to the Great Market Hall and offering a lively blend of international fashion boutiques, Hungarian designer shops, artisan craft stores, and souvenir stalls selling everything from paprika to Herend porcelain. The elegant 19th-century architecture, bustling cafés, and vibrant street life make it a must-visit for both tourists and locals.' },
  { img: 'bp-szimpla', name: "Szimpla Sunday Farmers' Market", link: 'https://szimpla-sunday-farmers-market.lucky-three-seven.com/', text: 'Every Sunday since 2012, Szimpla has hosted one of Budapest’s most beloved farmers’ markets. Each week, around 1,500–2,000 local and international visitors meet 30–40 farmers and producers, most of them from the region around Budapest. Visitors can discover a wide selection of cheeses, sausages, fruits, vegetables, spreads, honey, chutneys, juices, and organic as well as traditional delicacies. Upstairs, a market brunch is served, prepared with fresh ingredients from the market. The atmosphere is completed by live music, and every week a different NGO cooks for donations to support charitable causes.' },
  { img: 'shop-iringo', name: 'Iringó Virágmanufaktura – dried flower arrangements', meta: 'Károlyi utca 19., 1053 Budapest', link: 'https://iringo.com/', text: 'Iringó Virágmanufaktura is a unique flower workshop and boutique in the heart of Budapest, specializing exclusively in dried flower arrangements. Since its founding in 2008, this charming shop has become known for its creative, long-lasting wreaths, bouquets, table decorations, and seasonal ornaments—all handmade from flowers sourced from Hungarian farmers. These “immortal” creations can last for several years with proper care. Every item is uniquely designed and crafted by hand, making each piece a one-of-a-kind gift or home accessory.' },
  { img: 'shop-folkart', name: 'Folkart Handicraft House', meta: 'Régiposta utca 12., 1052 Budapest', link: 'https://folkartkezmuveshaz.hu/?lang=en', text: 'If you’re looking to bring home an authentic piece of Hungary, the folklore shop in Budapest’s 5th district (Belváros) is a must-visit. Since 2002, this shop has been offering a wide selection of handmade items such as ceramics, embroidery, lace, woven textiles, wood and bone carvings, dolls, and traditional costumes. Whether you are searching for a unique souvenir, a meaningful gift, or a piece of Hungarian tradition to take home, the Folkart Handicraft House provides a treasure trove of beautifully crafted items.' },
];

